  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1138 AM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nOverall, the progressive and somewhat active pattern continues\ninto the middle of next week, though New Mexico lies mainly in-\nbetween the polar and southern stream branches of the upper-level\njet stream. Thus the overall impact level stays relatively minor.\nForecast confidence decreases into early next week, but some\nguidance is picking up on another weather system crossing New\nMexico around next Wednesday into Thanksgiving Day; a little more\non that below...\n\nRecent guidance has dampened out the initial short-wave trough\nclipping northwestern and northern New Mexico on late Thursday\nnight into early Friday, with more energy now concentrated into a\ntrailing/follow-up trough, whose axis does not exit stage east \nuntil around 00Z/Sun. The net effect is a longer period of \nprecipitation chances, focused across the northern tier of the \nstate, especially the higher terrain. QPF remains paltry overall\n(with PW < 0.3 inches ahead of the system), though snow levels \nare projected to be lower than recent systems, falling to the \n6,000-6,500 ft. range as Saturday progresses. A dusting of snow \ncould fall in areas around Cuba on U. S. Hwy. 550, with even \nFarmington seeing a shot at some flurries. High-end (90th \npercentile) amounts high in the Tusas are in the 2-4 inch range, \nso advisories seem unlikely at this time. Windy conditions, but\nagain, sub-advisory, are still on tap for northeastern NM on\nFriday afternoon and then focused more in the central and southern\nhighlands on Saturday.\n\nModest zonal flow should make for a quiet weather day on Sunday.\nOf note for Sunday morning...the ABQ Sunport has a 70-80% chance\nof seeing its first freeze of the season (assuming we do not touch\nfreezing tonight). Timing of the early week system has shifted a\nbit later, to more in the Monday night-early Tuesday timeframe. \nModels are advertising mentionable PoPs for much of the area, but\nagain with low QPF amounts, with the system being somewhat \nmoisture-starved and quick-hitting, though it does appear to have \nsome left-exit region lift from a jet streak over Sonora coming \ninto play. Snow levels look similar to the Saturday system. During\nthe past couple weeks, there has been a tendency for model runs \nto pull back on precip chances as systems approach, so we shall \nsee...\n\nBeyond the official forecast period, some long-range guidance,\nmainly in the GFS/GEFS camp, is advertising a trough or closed low\ncutting across NM in the timeframe of next Wed into Thanksgiving\nDay, with H7 temps around -2 to -4C. Ensemble cluster analysis\nshows that only about 20% of 00Z solutions favor this scenario,\nwith the more likely outcome an open wave moving across northern\nNM yet again.",
